        "original_id": "de149440-6a89-5b65-8486-1b94a4024c12",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Sharmi Albrechtsen, Aksel Sandemose\n- Works: A Piece of Danish Happiness, A Fugitive Crosses His Tracks\n- Concepts: Law of Jante, Hygge, happiness in Denmark\n- Organizations: Gallup Poll, United Nations\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Aksel Sandemose created the Law of Jante as a concept in his novel \"A Fugitive Crosses His Tracks.\"\n- In 1936, the English translation of Aksel Sandemose's \"A Fugitive Crosses His Tracks\" was published in the United States.\n- Aksel Sandemose identified the Law of Jante as ten rules in \"A Fugitive Crosses His Tracks.\"\n- In 2012, a Gallup Poll taken by the United Nations was cited by some sources as considering Danish people the happiest in the world.\n- Sharmi Albrechtsen wrote \"A Piece of Danish Happiness.\"\n- Sharmi Albrechtsen's book \"A Piece of Danish Happiness\" explores why Danish people are considered the happiest in the world.\n- Sharmi Albrechtsen attributes Danish happiness to the cultural Law of Jante, a state of Hygge meaning being together comfortably, Denmark's welfare system, and factors intrinsic to being happy.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a584646f-12f1-5834-8d8c-1686cf64aa91",
        "text": "Page: Sharmi Albrechtsen. Sentence: She has written a book, A Piece of Danish Happiness, which explores why the Danish people are considered the happiest in the world, according to some sources, including the 2012 Gallup Poll taken by the United Nations.\nPage: Sharmi Albrechtsen. Sentence: Albrechtsen attributes this happiness to the cultural Laws of Jante, a state of “Hygge” meaning being together comfortably, Denmark’s welfare system, and factors intrinsic to being happy.\nPage: Law of Jante. Sentence: The Jante Law as a concept was created by the Dano-Norwegian author Aksel Sandemose, who, in his novel \"A Fugitive Crosses His Tracks\" (\"En flyktning krysser sitt spor\", 1933, English translation published in the USA in 1936), identified the Law of Jante as ten rules.",
